/**
	An audio_node_chain connects a list of nodes one after another, so that audio entering the chain passes through each of them in order before going to its endpoint.
	audio_node_chain(audio_node@ source = null, audio_node@ endpoint = null, audio_engine@ engine = sound_default_engine);
	## Arguments:
		* audio_node@ source = null: a node whose output is attached to the start of the chain, or null to attach something later.
		* audio_node@ endpoint = null: the node that the end of the chain is attached to, or null to set it later with `set_endpoint()`.
		* audio_engine@ engine = sound_default_engine: the engine to create the chain in.
	## Remarks:
		An audio_node_chain is an audio_node with one input and one output, and has all of the methods and properties of audio_node as well as the ones listed here. Attaching something to its input sends it through the chain, and attaching its output changes its endpoint.
		Connecting nodes one after another with `attach_output_bus()` works, but taking a node out of the middle of such a line means reconnecting its neighbours by hand. A chain does that for you, so effects can be inserted, removed and reordered while audio is playing.
		Every mixer and sound has a chain of its own, its `effects_chain`, which is the easiest way to give it effects. A chain of your own is useful for a set of effects that several things should share, such as one that sounds from a radio go through.
		Calling `add_node()` without saying where adds the node to the end of the chain.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	sound s;
	s.load("C:/Windows/media/ding.wav");
	audio_node_chain@ effects = s.effects_chain; // Every sound has a chain of its own.
	effects.add_node(audio_high_pass_filter(500, 2));
	effects.add_node(audio_low_pass_filter(3000, 2)); // Together, a tinny telephone sound.
	s.play_wait();
	effects.clear();
	s.play_wait(); // And the same sound without any effects.
}
